Function Round_income_1040( taxable_income )
Title: Round income for 1040 tax lookup
NodeLocation: 312,952,1
Description: Rounds income for tax table lookup. Although the tax computation for Form 1040 is essentially the tax bracket rate times the income that falls in that tax bracket, the tax tables add one complexity. If your taxable income is less that $3000, you need to "discretize" your income into $25 buckets and use the midpoint of that bucket, or for incomes between 3000 and 100K, you need to discretize the income into the appropriate $50 bucket and use the midpoint of that bucket before multiplying. For incomes above $100K, it simplifies to a straight multiplication with to rounding. This function rounds to the appropriate bucket centroid.~
~
For example, if your taxable income is $51,342, it falls in the bucket from $51,300 to $51,350. So you need to first round to $51,325 and then multiply by the corresponding tax rate(s).
NodeSize: 80,24
Definition: if Taxable_income<5 then 0~
else if Taxable_income<15 then 10~
else if Taxable_income<25 then 20~
else if taxable_income <3000 then floor(taxable_income /25)*25 + 12.5~
else if taxable_income <100k then floor(taxable_income /50)*50 + 25~
else taxable_income

Variable Rounded_taxable_inco
ValueState: 2,764,48,424,654,,MIDM
Title: Rounded taxable income
Units: $
NodeLocation: 408,472,1
Description: The taxable income after rounding to the centroid of the IRS tax table bin. Without this rounding, the simple computation differs from the IRS's tax due by a few dollars. For example, when your taxable income is $18,000 married filing jointly, your tax should be 10%. But the actual amount due is $1,803, rather than $1,800. This is because the $18,000 needs to be "rounded" to $18,025, the centroid of the appropriate bin in the Tax Tables, before multiplying by 10%. 
NodeSize: 56,32
Definition: Round_income_1040( Taxable_income )
Aliases: Alias Al1522879484

